    "narrative": "Today\u2019s offensive engine is anchored by **Corbin Carroll** (85.94), who remains the priority play as he leads off against Mikolas. With the opposing pitcher\u2019s delivery profile offering an 87% stolen base success rate and the park factor providing a significant boost, **Corbin Carroll** is in prime position to pad our scarcest category. **Gabriel Moreno** (70.65) also finds himself in an excellent spot against the same matchup, benefiting from a favorable number three hole and a soft 4.71 xERA from the mound. While **Austin Riley** (70.38) carries a lower base expectation, his massive 16% park factor bump against Baz makes him a top-tier utility option despite the lower-order volatility.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Travis Bazzana** (39.05), who narrowly missed our 40-point \"Zebras\" threshold. Despite a +7% platoon advantage against Rasmussen, the combination of a negative park factor and poor SP skill correlation kept him sidelined. We are also monitoring the performance of **Jose Caballero** (51.08), whose score is suppressed by a low batting order position and a tough matchup against the opposing battery, though his speed remains a constant threat. **Josh Naylor** (49.47) and **Mickey Moniak** (55.36) represent high-risk maneuvers today; both must contend with elite or near-elite opposing arms, specifically deGrom and Misiorowski, forcing us to temper expectations despite their talent. \n\nBeyond the starters, we are tracking progress on the injured front, where **Giancarlo Stanton** and **Trevor Story** remain on the IL, leaving our bench depth tested. While **James Wood** (67.79) provides a sturdy, high-floor presence at the top of the lineup, we are wary of the risks associated with the lighter production expected from **Tyler Soderstrom** (53.07), who faces a challenging lefty in Prielipp. We are moving forward with this configuration, prioritizing speed and high-leverage plate appearances to maintain our competitive edge in this 5x5 format.",
